IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc6-builders.l4 -- helpers shared by the modules of part ccc6 (Criminal Code, Part V, ss 150 to 182).
-- Every top-level name begins with a section label, as the naming rule of PLAN.md section 1 requires. No offence or rule of its own lives here.

§ `Punishment on summary conviction where the section states none`

-- Several offence sections say "or is guilty of an offence punishable on summary conviction" and state no penalty (for example s 160(1), s 162(5)(b), s 169(b)).
-- The penalty then comes from s 787(1), which is in another part (ccc29): PLAN.md section 6, seam [T]. The part takes that answer as an INPUT, a Punishment,
-- and these sections return it with the mode set to "on summary conviction" (so that a caller who passes a record with another mode does not get a wrong mode back).
-- src:3102 | 160 (1) Every person who commits bestiality is guilty of an indictable offence and liable to imprisonment for a term not exceeding ten years or is guilty of an offence punishable on summary conviction.
@ref Criminal Code s 160(1), one of the sections that state no summary penalty (the penalty is in s 787(1), another part) (src:3102)
GIVEN `s 787(1) the general penalty for an offence punishable on summary conviction` IS A Punishment
GIVETH A Punishment
`ss 150 to 182 the punishment on summary conviction that s 787(1) fixes` MEANS
    Punishment OF `on summary conviction`
                , `s 787(1) the general penalty for an offence punishable on summary conviction`'s `maximum imprisonment`
                , `s 787(1) the general penalty for an offence punishable on summary conviction`'s `minimum imprisonment`
                , `s 787(1) the general penalty for an offence punishable on summary conviction`'s `maximum fine`
                , `s 787(1) the general penalty for an offence punishable on summary conviction`'s `minimum fine`

§ `Where in the Code: a provision against a section number`

-- R1 of PLAN.md: a provision that names offences by section number is tested against the core record `Provision of the Code`.
-- "an offence under section 151" is any provision of section 151; "an offence under subsection 153(1)" is that subsection only.
GIVEN `the provision` IS A `Provision of the Code`
      `the section number` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`ss 150 to 182 the provision is in the section` MEANS
    `the provision`'s `section` EQUALS `the section number`

GIVEN `the provision` IS A `Provision of the Code`
      `the section number` IS A NUMBER
      `the subsection` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`ss 150 to 182 the provision is the subsection` MEANS
        `the provision`'s `section` EQUALS `the section number`
    AND `the provision`'s `subsection` EQUALS `the subsection`
